discussion_comments.comment_body:
**[Critical]** TOCTOU race: concurrent requests with the same `sessionId` can both pass this check before either session is created, then the second request's `loadCliConfig` calls `process.exit(1)` — terminating the shared ACP child and every session on its channel.

Failure scenario: Two concurrent `POST /session` requests with the same `sessionId` under `thread` scope (branch/worktree present, or explicit `sessionScope: "thread"`) both pass `sessionExistsInAnyState` (neither session exists yet). Bridge coalescing only applies under `single` scope, so both reach `doSpawn` → `loadCliConfig`. The first creates the session; the second finds it now exists and calls `process.exit(1)` at `config.ts:1990`, killing every session on the shared channel.

The route guard correctly handles the sequential duplicate case. The residual race is newly reachable because this PR introduces externally-supplied session IDs to the HTTP path — previously only `randomUUID()` was used, making duplicates impossible.

```suggestion
      requestedSessionId = rawSessionId;
      // Check for duplicate before touching the bridge
      if (
        await new SessionService(workspaceCwd).sessionExistsInAnyState(
          requestedSessionId,
        )
      ) {
        res.status(409).json({
          error: `Session "${requestedSessionId}" already exists`,
          code: 'session_id_conflict',
        });
        return;
      }
      // Reserve this id against concurrent duplicates
      if (inFlightSessionIds.has(requestedSessionId)) {
        res.status(409).json({
          error: `Session "${requestedSessionId}" creation already in progress`,
          code: 'session_id_conflict',
        });
        return;
      }
      inFlightSessionIds.add(requestedSessionId);
```

(Plus a `const inFlightSessionIds = new Set<string>()` at module scope and a `finally { inFlightSessionIds.delete(requestedSessionId) }` wrapping the spawn, or alternatively replace `process.exit(1)` in `loadCliConfig` with a thrown error when invoked from the ACP path.)

